# 2. Core Information Architecture Principles

## 2.1 One Product

Student, Mentor, and Admin are three role-based workplaces inside one GrowFlow product.

```text
                         GROWFLOW
                            |
              +-------------+-------------+
              |             |             |
            BUILD       SUPERVISE       GOVERN
           Student        Mentor         Admin
```

They share:

- identity
- global top navigation
- search
- notifications
- profile
- settings
- design language
- resource concepts
- authorization model
- event model
- platform services

Their workspace content and permissions differ.

---

# 3. Global Top Navigation — New Canonical Model

## 3.1 Top Navigation Exists Everywhere

The top navigation is a **global GrowFlow navigation layer**.

It exists on:

- Public website
- Authentication pages where appropriate
- Student workplace
- Mentor workplace
- Admin workplace
- Shared authenticated pages

The top bar is therefore not an application-only element.

It is part of the GrowFlow product identity.

---

# 4. Logged-Out Top Navigation

When the user is not authenticated, the top navigation should expose:

```text
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
| GrowFlow | Features | Documentation | Contact | Workplace | Login | Sign Up |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
```

The exact visual placement and spacing are deferred to the visual/UI phase.

The IA is fixed:

### Public navigation

- Home
- Features
- Documentation
- Contact

### Authentication actions

- Login
- Sign Up

### Workplace

- Opens the workplace selector/entry interaction.
- Logged-out users may see the three workplace identities.
- Entering a protected workplace requires authentication and authorization.

---

# 5. Login and Sign Up Are Separate Actions

The user specifically requested that Login and Sign Up remain separate.

Therefore:

```text
LOGIN
```

and

```text
SIGN UP
```

are independent top-nav actions.

They should not be collapsed into one generic "Get Started" control.

---

# 6. Login Routing

When a logged-out user clicks **Login**, the system must provide the correct role authentication path.

Recommended model:

```text
Login
  |
  +-- Student Login
  |
  +-- Mentor Login
  |
  +-- Admin
       protected / non-public entry
```

Canonical routes:

```text
/auth/student/sign-in
/auth/mentor/sign-in
```

Admin does not receive a public registration flow.

If the user's intended destination already establishes the required role, the login flow can route directly to that role's authentication page.

Example:

```text
/student/projects/123
       |
   not logged in
       |
       v
/auth/student/sign-in
       |
    success
       |
       v
/student/projects/123
```

---

# 7. Sign Up Routing

Sign Up remains separate from Login.

Recommended:

```text
Sign Up
  |
  +-- Student
  |
  +-- Mentor
```

Routes:

```text
/auth/student/register
/auth/mentor/register
```

Admin:

```text
NO PUBLIC ADMIN SIGN-UP
```

Admin access remains controlled/protected.

---

# 8. Workplace Button — Canonical Behavior

The **Workplace** button is the central role-navigation mechanism.

Clicking it opens a role/workplace selector.

Conceptually:

```text
                    [ Workplace ]
                          |
            +-------------+-------------+
            |             |             |
         Student        Mentor        Admin
          BUILD        SUPERVISE       GOVERN
```

The selector should communicate that these are **workplaces**, not three unrelated applications.

---

# 9. Workplace Button — Logged-Out Behavior

For a logged-out visitor:

```text
Workplace
   |
   +-- Student / BUILD
   |       |
   |       +--> Student Login
   |
   +-- Mentor / SUPERVISE
   |       |
   |       +--> Mentor Login
   |
   +-- Admin / GOVERN
           |
           +--> Protected Admin Entry
```

The visitor must not be given a public Admin registration option.

---

# 10. Workplace Button — Logged-In Behavior

For an authenticated user:

```text
Workplace
   |
   +-- Student / BUILD
   +-- Mentor / SUPERVISE
   +-- Admin / GOVERN
```

However:

> **Only authorized workplaces are actionable.**

The frontend should not imply access to a workplace that the backend authorization layer will reject.

If the account has only Student authorization:

```text
Workplace
  |
  +-- Student     AVAILABLE
  +-- Mentor      UNAVAILABLE
  +-- Admin       UNAVAILABLE
```

The UI may either hide unauthorized workplaces or show them as unavailable according to the final UX decision. The authorization rule itself is fixed: **the backend remains authoritative**.

---

# 11. Workplace Routing

Selecting a workplace routes the user to its canonical entry:

```text
Student / BUILD
    -> /student/dashboard

Mentor / SUPERVISE
    -> /mentor/overview

Admin / GOVERN
    -> /admin/overview
```

If the user is already inside that workplace, clicking the workplace item can return them to the workplace's primary landing page.

---

# 12. Profile in the Top Navigation

Profile is explicitly part of the global top navigation.

Authenticated state:

```text
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| GrowFlow | Public Links | Workplace | ... | Profile         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
```

Clicking Profile:

```text
Profile
   |
   +--> /profile
```

Profile remains global rather than being duplicated as a role-specific sidebar item.

---

# 13. Settings Relationship

Profile and Settings are separate concepts.

```text
Profile
   |
   +--> /profile

Settings
   |
   +--> /settings
```

Settings may contain:

```text
/settings/profile
/settings/preferences
```

but the exact settings taxonomy remains intentionally limited at this stage.

---

# 14. Authenticated Top Navigation

Once authenticated, the top bar becomes conceptually:

```text
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
| GrowFlow | ... | Workplace | Search | Notifications | Profile |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
```

The exact ordering can be finalized during UI architecture/design, but the IA requirement is:

- Global
- persistent
- consistent
- role-independent in geometry
- role-aware in destination
- authorization-aware

---

# 15. Top Navigation vs Sidebar

This is an important architectural distinction.

## Top Navigation

Global product-level controls:

- GrowFlow identity/logo
- public navigation where applicable
- Workplace selector
- Search
- Notifications
- Profile
- Login
- Sign Up

## Sidebar

Role/workspace-specific navigation.

Example:

```text
TOP NAV
------------------------------------------------
GrowFlow | Workplace | Search | Notifications | Profile

SIDEBAR
------------------------------------------------
Student:
Dashboard
Projects
```

Mentor:

```text
Overview
Groups
Students
Projects
Project Definitions
Project Instances
At Risk
AI Mentor
```

Admin:

```text
Overview
Mentors
Students
Groups
Projects
AI Observatory
Cost & Usage
System Health
Documents & RAG
Security & Audit
Analytics
```

This is the recommended final relationship.

